        </div>

        <div class="space-y-4 mb-6">
            <!-- Acts 20:7 -->
            <div class="bg-white p-5 rounded border border-slate-200">
                <span class="font-bold text-caci-minister text-base block mb-1">
                    <a href="https://www.blueletterbible.org/kjv/act/20/6" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Acts 20:6&ndash;12 (The Troas Meeting) &nearr;</a>
                </span>
                <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 italic mb-3">
                    &ldquo;And upon the first day of the week, when the disciples came together to break bread, Paul preached unto them, ready to depart on the morrow; and continued his speech until midnight...&rdquo;
                </p>
                <div class="bg-parchment p-3 rounded text-xs text-slate-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
                    <p><strong>Contextual Facts:</strong></p>
                    <p>&bull; <strong>Verse 6:</strong> They sailed after the Days of Unleavened Bread. Paul was rushing to reach Jerusalem in time for the Feast of Pentecost (v. 16).</p>
                    <p>&bull; <strong>Greek Text:</strong> The word for &ldquo;week&rdquo; is <em>sabbaton</em> (plural Sabbaths). In Greek: <em>mia ton sabbaton</em> (&ldquo;on the first of the Sabbaths&rdquo; &mdash; referring to the 7-Sabbath count between Firstfruits and Pentecost per Leviticus 23:15&ndash;16!).</p>
                    <p>&bull; In biblical timekeeping, the &ldquo;first day&rdquo; begins at Saturday sunset. Paul met Saturday night after Sabbath, broke bread, spoke until dawn, and then walked 19 miles to Assos on Sunday morning.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- 1 Corinthians 16:1-3 -->
            <div class="bg-white p-5 rounded border border-slate-200">
                <span class="font-bold text-caci-minister text-base block mb-1">
                    <a href="https://www.blueletterbible.org/kjv/1co/16/1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">1 Corinthians 16:1&ndash;3 (The Jerusalem Collection) &nearr;</a>
                </span>
                <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 italic mb-3">
                    &ldquo;Upon the first day of the week let every one of you lay by him in store, as God hath prospered him, that there be no gatherings when I come.&rdquo;
                </p>
                <div class="bg-parchment p-3 rounded text-xs text-slate-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
                    <p><strong>Contextual Facts:</strong></p>
                    <p>&bull; This was not a corporate church offering passed in a plate during a service. Paul instructed each person to store provisions <em>at home</em> (&ldquo;lay by him in store&rdquo;).</p>
                    <p>&bull; This was food relief gathered for the famine-stricken saints in Jerusalem, timed around the Feast of Weeks.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-slate-50 border-t-2 border-caci-tone2 p-4 rounded-b-lg text-sm text-slate-800 leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-caci-minister">Key Understanding:</strong>
            Neither Acts 20:7 nor 1 Corinthians 16:2 describes a regular weekly Sunday church service replacing the Sabbath. When understood in their biblical Feast calendar context, they confirm the apostles' ongoing covenant practice.
        </div>
    </section>

