    <!-- 13. HISTORICAL SHIFT: CONSTANTINE & ROME -->
    <section id="history" class="card p-6 md:p-8 scroll-mt-24 border-l-8 border-l-caci-red">
        <div class="border-b border-slate-200 pb-4 mb-6">
            <span class="badge badge-urgent mb-1">Church History &amp; Roman Decrees</span>
            <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold">13. If Scripture Didn't Change the Sabbath to Sunday, Who Did?</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-4 mb-6">
            <div class="bg-white p-5 rounded border border-slate-200">
                <span class="font-bold text-caci-minister text-base block mb-1">
                    The Civil Edict of Roman Emperor Constantine I (March 7, 321 AD)
                </span>
                <blockquote class="scripture-quote text-slate-700 text-xs md:text-sm italic leading-relaxed">
                    &ldquo;On the venerable Day of the Sun let the magistrates and people residing in cities rest, and let all workshops be closed.&rdquo;
                    <span class="block font-semibold text-caci-gray mt-1">&mdash; Codex Justinianus, lib. 3, tit. 12, 3</span>
                </blockquote>
                <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 mt-2">
                    Sunday was a regular workday in the Roman Empire until Emperor Constantine, an adherent of Sol Invictus (the Unconquered Sun), issued the first civil law mandating Sunday rest.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-white p-5 rounded border border-slate-200">
                <span class="font-bold text-caci-minister text-base block mb-1">
                    The Council of Laodicea (363&ndash;365 AD): Canon 29
                </span>
                <blockquote class="scripture-quote text-slate-700 text-xs md:text-sm italic leading-relaxed">
                    &ldquo;Christians must not judaize by resting on the Sabbath, but must work on that day, rather honouring the Lord's Day; and, if they can, resting then as Christians. But if any shall be found to be judaizers, let them be anathema from Christ.&rdquo;
                </blockquote>
                <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 mt-2">
                    Under penalty of excommunication (anathema), imperial church councils officially outlawed seventh-day Sabbath rest and substituted Sunday.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-slate-50 border-t-2 border-caci-red p-4 rounded-b-lg text-sm text-slate-800 leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-caci-minister">Key Understanding:</strong>
            The change from Saturday Sabbath to Sunday rest was never commanded by God, Messiah, or the Apostles. It was instituted centuries later by pagan Rome and imperial ecumenical councils.
        </div>
    </section>

